5. Rewrite using var where appropriate:
ArrayList<HashMap<String, List<Integer>>> data = new ArrayList<HashMap<String, List<Integer>>>();
HashMap<String, List<Integer>> entry = new HashMap<String, List<Integer>>();
List<Integer> values = new ArrayList<Integer>();

items.a:
var data   = new ArrayList<HashMap<String, List<Integer>>>();
var entry  = new HashMap<String, List<Integer>>();
var values = new ArrayList<Integer>();

var eliminates repetition of the type on the right-hand side when it is already explicit from the constructor call. The type is still fully inferred and statically checked by the compiler — no type safety is lost. The code becomes significantly shorter and easier to read, especially for deeply nested generics. This is exactly the use case var was designed for.

